# Production worker target profile: TX2 (K7A).
#
# Reviewed, non-secret host parameters only. The lifecycle scripts
# (`install.sh` / `verify.sh` / `reconcile.sh`) are target-agnostic and read this
# file through `load_target_profile`, so preparing another host means adding a
# profile, never a second lifecycle implementation.
#
# No credential may ever be added here. The TCR pull credential reaches the host
# only through the protected production environment, and only into the execution
# context that Komodo/Periphery uses (root Docker).

## The target name, which must equal the directory name and the staged runtime
## selector (`reconcile.sh <target> ...`).
WORKER_TARGET=tx2

## The host's existing mutation lock root, owned by TX2's deploy owner. K7A
## serializes with every other mutation of that host through
## `/opt/wotb-tx2/.deploy.lock`; no independent worker-only lock exists, and the
## worker never creates or replaces the lock file. TX2 currently hosts no
## production workload, so this is not a second host-management path.
: "${WORKER_LOCK_ROOT:=/opt/wotb-tx2}"

## SHA-scoped SCP staging root, deliberately under the host's deploy-owned root so
## SCP never needs a privileged write directly under /opt (the TX2 SSH account
## `ubuntu` is not root).
: "${WORKER_STAGING_ROOT:=/opt/wotb-tx2/production-worker}"

## sudo = the SSH account is not root; the reconcile re-executes itself through
## non-interactive sudo, preserving ONLY the registry credential that this phase
## owns (through the environment, never through argv, disk, or a log).
WORKER_PRIVILEGE=sudo

## The reviewed Docker Compose package. The contract is capability-based ("Docker
## Compose v2 compatible with current Komodo runtime operations"), so no Compose
## version is pinned here: the distribution package is the reviewed owner, exactly
## as the host's own package policy requires.
WORKER_COMPOSE_PACKAGE=docker-compose-v2

## The reviewed Docker Hub mirror. TX2 cannot reach registry-1.docker.io directly
## (K5 measured the timeout), so this in-network mirror is a hard prerequisite for
## any future workload that pulls a public base image. `install.sh` owns this
## value and refuses any other daemon setting.
WORKER_DOCKER_HUB_MIRROR=https://mirror.ccs.tencentyun.com

## The private WotBTools image used to prove authenticated access. The repository
## and tag come from current main: every TX owner publishes
## `<registry>/<namespace>/wotbtools-<service>` from main, and the frontend tag
## `latest` is published only from current main. The worker resolves the full
## reference from the workflow's reviewed registry variables, never from a
## hardcoded host name.
WORKER_VERIFY_IMAGE_REPOSITORY=wotbtools-frontend
WORKER_VERIFY_IMAGE_TAG=latest

## The host's reviewed WireGuard identity. K7A never modifies WireGuard; it only
## proves the address is still present so a future TX2 workload can reach the TX1
## service plane.
WORKER_WIREGUARD_ADDRESS=10.20.0.3

## Service-plane endpoints a future TX2 frontend workload must be able to reach,
## with the K6B values frozen in Git: Business API, Keycloak, and the Yecao AI
## service. K7A proves reachability only; it changes no endpoint.
WORKER_REQUIRED_ENDPOINTS="10.20.0.1:8087 10.20.0.1:8080 10.20.0.2:8089"

## The readiness token this host emits only when every worker check passed. It is
## deliberately host-scoped so it can never be confused with the TX1 production
## topology token (`TX_RUNTIME_READY`), which belongs to the TX runtime gate.
WORKER_READY_TOKEN=TX2_PRODUCTION_WORKER_READY

## The existing systemd unit owned by the Periphery lifecycle. K7A never writes
## it; it only proves the agent is still healthy and still not an inbound server.
WORKER_PERIPHERY_UNIT=periphery

## The Periphery state K7A must leave in place: identity, Core trust and the
## agent's own configuration all belong to the Periphery lifecycle, so the worker
## only re-proves that they are still present after its own reconciliation.
WORKER_PERIPHERY_STATE="/etc/komodo/keys/periphery.key /etc/komodo/keys/core.pub /etc/komodo/periphery.config.toml"
